 "worked_example": {
  "byte1": 130,
  "why": "the finding's own best illustration, and a correction to how I first summarised it. I wrote \"372 objects read SR130 alone and say 52\" as though it were one population. It is FOUR keys: (width 4, byte3 6) with 273 objects and (width 8, byte3 56) with 99 both give 52 - two forms of the register read the same functional way, agreeing, which is corroboration rather than one row - while (width 8, byte3 32) gives 50 on 9 objects and (width 8, byte3 64) gives 10 on 4. Every one of the four has decode_sr reporting sr=130. Summing across keys is exactly the collapse this census exists to undo, so quoting the sum without the split was the coarse-key mistake in miniature. Named by the linker lane, which read the rows rather than the summary"
 },
 "not_claimed": [
  "which BITS of byte3 carry the register: 0x38, 0x20 and 0x40 differ in bits 3, 4 and 6, so the key is the whole byte",
  "anything about registers absent from this population",
  "that the FORM COUNT here is comparable with any other count of SR130-sharing forms. This population admits only objects with exactly one read and one entry - 10,661 of 22,037 - so it deliberately excludes every multi-register program. Within it, byte1 0x82 appears as FOUR distinct (byte3, width) forms. The release record separately names THIRTEEN latent SR130-sharing forms, counted over a broader population that includes multi-register objects. Four and thirteen are counts over different populations: they do not conflict and must never be summed or compared",
  "that a slot-29 entry is sufficient to author a section: this is the entry value only, and the vector length and section size are measured elsewhere"
 ],
